    {
      "title": "Subqueries and CTEs: The Mental Model",
      "role": "outline",
      "outlineIndex": 0,
      "pages": [
        {
          "title": "Subqueries and CTEs: The Mental Model",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "1. What a Subquery Is",
              "ink": "green"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "A ==subquery== is a `SELECT` nested inside another statement",
                "Inner result acts as a {blue:value}, a {green:list} or a {purple:table}",
                "**Scalar** = exactly one row, one column; fits wherever an expression fits"
              ],
              "style": "dot",
              "frame": "dashed",
              "ink": "green"
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "lang": "sql",
              "lines": [
                "SELECT name, (SELECT MAX(salary) FROM emp) AS top",
                "FROM emp;"
              ],
              "note": "scalar subquery in the SELECT list"
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "2. Placement Decides Shape",
              "ink": "blue"
            },
            {
              "type": "table",
              "headers": [
                "Where",
                "Takes"
              ],
              "rows": [
                [
                  "`SELECT` / `WHERE`",
                  "scalar or list"
                ],
                [
                  "`FROM`",
                  "table"
                ],
                [
                  "CTE",
                  "table, referenced ==many times=="
                ]
              ],
              "frame": "dashed"
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "3. Derived Table vs CTE",
              "ink": "purple"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "**Derived table**: a subquery in `FROM`, named by an alias",
                "**CTE**: `WITH name AS (...)`, valid only for the ==one statement== after it"
              ],
              "style": "dot"
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "lang": "sql",
              "lines": [
                "SELECT d.dept, d.a",
                "FROM (SELECT dept, AVG(salary) a FROM emp GROUP BY dept) d;"
              ],
              "note": "derived table with alias d"
            },
            {
              "type": "callout",
              "style": "sticky",
              "title": "Alias on a derived table",
              "text": "Required in ==most engines==. PostgreSQL 16+ and SQLite do not require one."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "IN, EXISTS and Correlated Subqueries",
      "role": "outline",
      "outlineIndex": 1,
      "pages": [
        {
          "title": "IN, EXISTS and Correlated Subqueries",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "1. IN and EXISTS",
              "ink": "green"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "`IN` compares a value against the ==list== the subquery returns",
                "`WHERE dept_id IN (SELECT id FROM dept WHERE city='Pune')`",
                "`EXISTS` is true if the subquery yields ==at least one row==",
                "Selected columns are ignored, so `SELECT 1` is conventional"
              ],
              "style": "dot",
              "frame": "dashed",
              "ink": "green"
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "2. Correlated Subqueries",
              "ink": "blue"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "Refers to a column of the **outer** query",
                "Logically ==re-evaluated per outer row=="
              ],
              "style": "dot"
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "lang": "sql",
              "lines": [
                "SELECT name, salary FROM emp e1",
                "WHERE salary > (",
                "  SELECT AVG(salary) FROM emp e2",
                "  WHERE e2.dept_id = e1.dept_id);"
              ],
              "note": "e1 is the outer row: employees above their own dept average"
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "3. Choosing and Anti-join",
              "ink": "purple"
            },
            {
              "type": "table",
              "headers": [
                "",
                "IN",
                "EXISTS"
              ],
              "rows": [
                [
                  "Works by",
                  "builds or probes a list",
                  "{green:stops at first match}"
                ],
                [
                  "NULLs",
                  "tricky",
                  "{green:NULL-safe}"
                ],
                [
                  "Best for",
                  "small static lists, readable",
                  "correlated checks"
                ]
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "Anti-join: ==NOT EXISTS== finds customers with no orders"
              ],
              "style": "check"
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "lang": "sql",
              "lines": [
                "SELECT c.id FROM customers c",
                "WHERE NOT EXISTS (",
                "  SELECT 1 FROM orders o",
                "  WHERE o.cust_id = c.id);"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "callout",
              "style": "sticky",
              "title": "Prefer NOT EXISTS",
              "text": "A single ==NULL== in a `NOT IN` list makes the whole filter return no rows. `NOT EXISTS` is safe."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "CTEs and Recursive CTEs",
      "role": "outline",
      "outlineIndex": 2,
      "pages": [
        {
          "title": "CTEs and Recursive CTEs",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "1. CTE basics",
              "ink": "green"
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "lang": "sql",
              "lines": [
                "WITH high AS (SELECT * FROM emp WHERE salary > 50000),",
                "     b AS (SELECT * FROM dept)",
                "SELECT * FROM high;"
              ],
              "note": "chain several with commas, one WITH"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "Prefer over a nested derived table: ==name each step==, reuse it many times",
                "{red:No} guarantee of materialization: the **engine** decides"
              ],
              "style": "dot"
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "2. Recursive CTE",
              "ink": "blue"
            },
            {
              "type": "diagram",
              "kind": "flow",
              "spec": {
                "nodes": [
                  {
                    "text": "Anchor",
                    "sub": "start rows",
                    "ink": "green"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "UNION ALL",
                    "ink": "blue"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Recursive member",
                    "sub": "reads the CTE",
                    "ink": "purple"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Stop",
                    "sub": "no rows returned",
                    "ink": "red"
                  }
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "lang": "sql",
              "lines": [
                "WITH RECURSIVE t AS (",
                "  SELECT id, boss_id, 1 AS lvl FROM emp WHERE boss_id IS NULL",
                "  UNION ALL",
                "  SELECT e.id, e.boss_id, t.lvl+1",
                "  FROM emp e JOIN t ON e.boss_id = t.id",
                "  WHERE t.lvl < 10",
                ")",
                "SELECT * FROM t;"
              ],
              "note": "org chart with a depth cap"
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "3. Cycles",
              "ink": "red"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "Cyclic data loops ==forever==",
                "Fix: depth cap or the engine's cycle limit",
                "`UNION ALL` does **not** stop cycles"
              ],
              "style": "cross"
            },
            {
              "type": "sidebox",
              "title": "SQL Server",
              "items": [
                "No `RECURSIVE` keyword",
                "Default limit: ==100 levels=="
              ],
              "ink": "orange"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "UNION vs UNION ALL and Choosing a Form",
      "role": "outline",
      "outlineIndex": 3,
      "pages": [
        {
          "title": "UNION vs UNION ALL and Choosing a Form",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "1. UNION vs UNION ALL",
              "ink": "green"
            },
            {
              "type": "table",
              "headers": [
                "",
                "UNION",
                "UNION ALL"
              ],
              "rows": [
                [
                  "Duplicates",
                  "{red:removed}",
                  "{green:kept}"
                ],
                [
                  "Extra step",
                  "sort or hash",
                  "none"
                ],
                [
                  "Speed",
                  "slower",
                  "==faster=="
                ],
                [
                  "Use when",
                  "you need distinct rows",
                  "duplicates impossible or wanted"
                ]
              ],
              "highlightRow": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "2. Rules for Combining",
              "ink": "blue"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "Same ==column count== in both SELECTs",
                "Types compatible **by position**",
                "Column names come from the ==first SELECT==",
                "One `ORDER BY` for the whole result, at the very end"
              ],
              "style": "check",
              "frame": "dashed",
              "ink": "blue"
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "lang": "sql",
              "lines": [
                "SELECT name, city FROM emp",
                "UNION ALL",
                "SELECT cname, town FROM customer",
                "ORDER BY name;"
              ],
              "note": "result columns are name, city; ORDER BY goes last"
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "3. Choosing a Form",
              "ink": "purple"
            },
            {
              "type": "table",
              "headers": [
                "You need",
                "Use"
              ],
              "rows": [
                [
                  "A single value",
                  "scalar subquery"
                ],
                [
                  "A membership test",
                  "`IN`"
                ],
                [
                  "An existence test",
                  "`EXISTS`"
                ],
                [
                  "Reused or readable steps",
                  "CTE"
                ],
                [
                  "A hierarchy",
                  "recursive CTE"
                ]
              ],
              "frame": "dashed"
            },
            {
              "type": "callout",
              "style": "sticky",
              "title": "Default",
              "text": "Reach for ==UNION ALL== first. Use `UNION` only when you must drop duplicates."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Common Mistakes with Subqueries and Set Operators",
      "role": "outline",
      "outlineIndex": 4,
      "pages": [
        {
          "title": "Common Mistakes with Subqueries and Set Operators",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "1. Silent wrong results",
              "ink": "red"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "`NOT IN` with one NULL in the list returns ==zero rows== (`x <> NULL` is unknown)",
                "Fix: `NOT EXISTS`, or filter NULLs out of the subquery",
                "`UNION` where `UNION ALL` fits: needless sort, ==drops real duplicates=="
              ],
              "style": "cross",
              "ink": "red"
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "lang": "sql",
              "lines": [
                "SELECT c.id FROM cust c",
                "WHERE NOT EXISTS (",
                "  SELECT 1 FROM orders o",
                "  WHERE o.cust_id = c.id",
                ");"
              ],
              "note": "NULL-safe anti-join"
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "2. Errors",
              "ink": "orange"
            },
            {
              "type": "table",
              "headers": [
                "Mistake",
                "Result",
                "Fix"
              ],
              "rows": [
                [
                  "Scalar subquery returns >1 row",
                  "runtime error",
                  "unique key, `MAX()`, `LIMIT 1`"
                ],
                [
                  "`FROM (SELECT ...)` without alias",
                  "fails in most engines (not PG 16+, SQLite)",
                  "`FROM (SELECT ...) d`"
                ]
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "3. Endless recursion",
              "ink": "purple"
            },
            {
              "type": "table",
              "headers": [
                "Recursive CTE form",
                "Cyclic data"
              ],
              "rows": [
                [
                  "`UNION ALL`, no cap",
                  "{red:loops forever}"
                ],
                [
                  "`UNION`, identical rows repeat",
                  "can stop a simple cycle"
                ],
                [
                  "`UNION` + depth counter",
                  "{red:still loops}: every row is distinct"
                ],
                [
                  "Depth cap, path check, PG 14+ `CYCLE`",
                  "{green:stops}"
                ]
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "callout",
              "style": "sticky",
              "title": "UNION ALL is not a loop guard",
              "text": "It is chosen for speed (SQL Server requires it). Always add ==WHERE t.lvl < 10==."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Subqueries, CTEs and Set Operators Cheat Sheet",
      "role": "outline",
